WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:10.040
Meselson and Stahl grew E. coli on heavy nitrogen, so all of its DNA was heavy.

2
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.340
Then they added ordinary nitrogen and let the cells grow.

3
00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:21.400
They spun the DNA in caesium chloride, where heavy, hybrid and light DNA settle in

4
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:27.000
separate bands. After one generation, all of the DNA sat in a single band.

5
00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:29.900
It was halfway between heavy and light.

6
00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:31.700
Why halfway?

7
00:00:32.533 --> 00:00:35.053
Three ideas could explain it.

8
00:00:36.113 --> 00:00:40.633
Conservative copying keeps the old double helix whole and builds a new one.

9
00:00:41.973 --> 00:00:46.913
Semiconservative copying gives each daughter one old strand and one new strand.

10
00:00:48.173 --> 00:00:52.813
Dispersive copying makes each daughter a patchwork of old and new pieces.

11
00:00:53.673 --> 00:00:57.233
Old strands are purple here, and new strands are blue.

12
00:00:58.083 --> 00:01:00.703
Now predict the first generation.

13
00:01:02.283 --> 00:01:06.703
Conservative copying gives a heavy band and a light band, and never a hybrid.

14
00:01:07.543 --> 00:01:10.803
The single hybrid band that was seen rules it out.

15
00:01:12.003 --> 00:01:14.903
Semiconservative copying predicts that hybrid band.

16
00:01:15.523 --> 00:01:16.923
So does dispersive copying.

17
00:01:17.903 --> 00:01:20.463
One generation cannot tell those two apart.

18
00:01:21.233 --> 00:01:23.833
So the second generation decides.

19
00:01:24.833 --> 00:01:29.853
Semiconservative copying splits each hybrid into one hybrid and one light

20
00:01:29.853 --> 00:01:33.793
molecule. That gives equal amounts of hybrid and light.

21
00:01:34.653 --> 00:01:40.193
If mixing is even, dispersive copying makes every molecule a quarter old.

22
00:01:40.853 --> 00:01:44.653
It predicts one band, a quarter of the way from light to heavy.

23
00:01:45.893 --> 00:01:49.493
Meselson and Stahl saw hybrid and light in equal amounts.

24
00:01:49.493 --> 00:01:52.533
Uniform dispersive copying is out.

25
00:01:53.367 --> 00:01:57.527
Now count a third generation of semiconservative copying.

26
00:01:58.327 --> 00:02:00.407
Start with one heavy duplex.

27
00:02:01.247 --> 00:02:03.307
After one round there are two hybrids.

28
00:02:04.267 --> 00:02:07.987
After two rounds there are two hybrids and two light molecules.

29
00:02:08.987 --> 00:02:12.907
After three rounds there are two hybrids and six light molecules.

30
00:02:13.747 --> 00:02:16.287
The two heavy strands never multiply.

31
00:02:16.287 --> 00:02:22.847
So for semiconservative copying, that is 25% hybrid, and 75% light.

32
00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:24.950
Here is the catch.

33
00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:29.430
The average heavy content is the same in all three models.

34
00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:34.130
Only the way the label is shared among molecules tells them apart.

35
00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:39.610
So predict the bands, and look at the generation where the models disagree.

36
00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:46.910
Keep this rule. Each new helix holds one parental strand and one new strand.
